<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sierra Leone Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1808">1808</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> Company was a British chartered company founded in 1791 by abolitionists to run a settlement for freed Black people on the Sierra Leone peninsula. It took over the failed Province of Freedom, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> in 1792 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-loyalists">Black Loyalists</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>, and handed the colony to the Crown in 1808.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The company grew out of an earlier venture. In 1787 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-for-the-relief-of-the-black-poor">Committee for the Relief of the Black Poor</a> sent 411 people, among them 300 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>'s Black Poor, many of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-loyalists">Black Loyalists</a>, and 60 English working-class women, to found a free settlement; they built Granville Town, which disease and abandonment wasted and the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temne-people">Temne people</a> burned in 1789. The St. George's Bay Company, founded in 1790, re-established Granville Town in 1791 for the 60 remaining Old Settlers.</p><p>Both ventures were promoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granville-sharp">Granville Sharp</a>, whose prospectus of 1790, Free English Territory in AFRICA, said the subscribers sought no present profit but a charitable measure. Early subscribers from the Clapham Sect included Henry Thornton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce">William Wilberforce</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-clarkson">Thomas Clarkson</a>, Thomas Gisbourne and Samuel Whitbread. The attorney general, Archibald Macdonald, blocked a royal charter, and slave-trade merchants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> and Lancaster petitioned against incorporation by act of parliament. Thornton nevertheless guided the bill through, and on 30 May 1791 it passed by 87 votes to 9.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1792 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-clarkson">John Clarkson</a> led over 1100 Black settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halifax-nova-scotia">Halifax, Nova Scotia</a>, most of them former slaves of the American colonies freed by the British during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>. These Nova Scotian Settlers founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> in March 1792, and their determination made it take shape and survive. The company issued a decimal currency of dollars and cents, struck by Matthew Boulton at the Soho Mint and dated 1791 and 1796.</p><p>When war broke out between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the company lost supplies and ships to privateers and the French navy, and in September 1794 the French burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> in a raid. The colonists rebuilt, helped by American merchants who sold the company vital supplies. The acting governor, Zachary Macaulay, and his council agreed to accept American Black settlers with a gift of land if they brought letters of good character from their clergy, became British subjects, paid their own passage and cleared a third of their land within two years. The company's ships traded along the coast, sending the Domingo to the River Gabon for wax, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and redwood in 1794.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sierra Leone Company” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The survivors regrouped, and the <b>Sierra Leone Company</b> took over the project, founding the Sierra Leone Colony and Protectorate at Freetown in 1792.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-freedom">Province of Freedom</a></li><li><blockquote>After the failure of the Province of Freedom, the <b>Sierra Leone Company</b> brought about 1,100 Black Loyalists from Nova Scotia under John Clarkson, who founded Freetown in 1792.</blockquote><a href="/state/sierra-leone-colony-and-protectorate">Sierra Leone Colony and Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
